WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:05.239
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.239 --> 00:00:08.489
I've realized I'm weird.

00:00:08.489 --> 00:00:13.191
I'm especially weird
when it comes to food.

00:00:13.191 --> 00:00:17.267
This is a picture of my kitchen cabinet.

00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:19.594
It sort of looks like I'm
hosting a gathering for

00:00:19.594 --> 00:00:22.520
chocolate fictional characters, I'm not.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:23.640
&gt;&gt; And no,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.230
I'm not super prepared for Easter.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.115
This bunny's been up here for
almost a year.

00:00:28.115 --> 00:00:29.520
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.590
&gt;&gt; What else makes me weird?

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.837
I scrape out every last grain
of rice from a bowl I eat.

00:00:34.837 --> 00:00:38.517
I spend extra time chopping
around the end of an onion.

00:00:38.517 --> 00:00:43.491
And I must have smelt the milk in my
fridge at least six times this morning to

00:00:43.491 --> 00:00:47.758
see if I could drink it,
versus having to pour it down the sink.

00:00:49.581 --> 00:00:52.163
I wish I knew why I did all these things,

00:00:52.163 --> 00:00:56.478
it's certainly not because I didn't
have enough to eat as a kid.

00:00:56.478 --> 00:00:58.362
Instead, I don't know,

00:00:58.362 --> 00:01:03.503
I feel some sort of obsessive obligation
to be mindful of the food I eat,

00:01:03.503 --> 00:01:08.755
to respect where it came from, and
to not waste any of it unnecessarily.

00:01:08.755 --> 00:01:15.930
To me, food is secret, which is why

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:21.490
when I found out about the amount of ways
that still exists, I was heart broken.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:29.685
I found that the equivalent to $165
billion is thrown away in the US

00:01:29.685 --> 00:01:35.256
every year from food that is
otherwise completely edible.

00:01:35.256 --> 00:01:41.620
$165 billion, imagine what else
we could do with all this money.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.970
This led me to ask, where on Earth
does all this food waste come from?

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:55.950
And the answer is sadly, from pretty much
everywhere, it starts in the fields.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.770
Consumer demand may change from crop
to plantage to when they're harvested.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.108
Which means that stores and

00:02:03.108 --> 00:02:06.140
distributors no longer want all
the food that's been grown.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:08.985
Often this can't find a new
home at short notice and

00:02:08.985 --> 00:02:11.510
it's left to rot
unharvested in the fields.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.789
Once harvested, food is taken
to facilities where it's sorted.

00:02:16.789 --> 00:02:20.781
Here, estimates vary by between 20 and

00:02:20.781 --> 00:02:28.434
50% of all produce is simply discarded
due to blemishes or being misshapen.

00:02:28.434 --> 00:02:32.336
Now I don't know about you but
once a carrot is cut up in my salad, or

00:02:32.336 --> 00:02:37.080
a strawberry is blended in my smoothie I
don't know, and I certainly don't care

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:40.695
what it started looking like.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:40.695 --> 00:02:43.159
&gt;&gt; Pausing here for just a moment,

00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:48.543
the fact that so much of our beautiful,
fresh produce is simply thrown

00:02:48.543 --> 00:02:54.030
away because it's ugly, not only
astounds me, but it also angers me.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.520
Luckily, I'm not the only one who
is completely outraged by this,

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.590
and there are start-ups that will rescue
these apparently ugly vegetables from

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:06.316
going to the landfill, and
will send them to your door instead.

00:03:06.316 --> 00:03:09.180
Check out imperfectproduce.com.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.300
&gt;&gt; If you too are not judgmental about how

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:16.046
your mashed potatoes started looking.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:16.046 --> 00:03:17.267
&gt;&gt; Continuing on journey

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.450
through the food distribution system,

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.958
we've reached the cities where the food
is set to be sold and, hopefully, eaten.

00:03:23.958 --> 00:03:27.989
Here some go to the restaurants and
the rest to grocery stores.

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:32.100
And, unsurprisingly,
food waste exists here, as well.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.310
But this is the step where you and I,

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.599
everyday consumers,
can have a daily impact.

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:46.220
Lets start with restaurants, a little fun
fact to insert here is that I'm British,

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.349
and one of my favorite things about
moving to the US is the fact that

00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:57.061
all restaurants no matter how fancy or
cheap will have to go containers.

00:03:57.061 --> 00:04:02.300
The fact that I love to go containers and
the associated leftovers.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:06.310
Really it shouldn't surprise you at this
point, as you know, I'm good with food.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:09.360
But even for me there are some times and

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.500
some dishes that I just don't want
to have again, or don't eat well.

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:18.667
As a show of hands, how many people
have thought about taking food home from

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:22.324
a restaurant, but decided that they
didn't like it enough to want to bother?

00:04:25.493 --> 00:04:30.055
And then for those of us who have brought
food home with the best intentions of

00:04:30.055 --> 00:04:34.921
eating it, how many of you have ended up
throwing these leftovers away uneaten?

00:04:37.151 --> 00:04:40.483
Yeah, this happens to me a bunch too.

00:04:40.483 --> 00:04:43.950
I guess it comes with the territory of
taking pretty much everything with me.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.710
Fundamentally, I believe that the mass
prevalence of to-go containers,

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:54.880
connects with one of my less favorite
things, the portion sizes in the US.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:59.880
And I don't want to give the impression
that this is an issue unique to the US,

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:05.270
but with us millennials eating out more
often than any generation before us.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:09.344
We ought to be more aware about how
much food waste we generates from

00:05:09.344 --> 00:05:11.758
restaurant's hearty portion sizes.

00:05:14.347 --> 00:05:17.228
Shifting now to think about
food waste in the home,

00:05:17.228 --> 00:05:19.850
I'd like to share another
story with you all.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:24.455
Not long after coming to Business School,
I decided I was going to start

00:05:24.455 --> 00:05:28.385
snacking healthier, so
I bought a big multipack of yogurt.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:34.602
Unfortunately cheap short chocolate
are always far more appealing to me than

00:05:34.602 --> 00:05:36.130
the yogurt in my fridge.

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:40.324
So as the expiration date started
looming towards me, I realized,

00:05:40.324 --> 00:05:43.574
there was no way I was going
to be able to eat it at all.

00:05:43.574 --> 00:05:45.085
But what was I to do?

00:05:45.085 --> 00:05:49.752
We're taught children not to accept
candy from strangers No person in my

00:05:49.752 --> 00:05:54.891
apartment building was going to want
yogurt from the random girl down the hall.

00:05:54.891 --> 00:05:56.714
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:56.714 --> 00:05:58.964
&gt;&gt; Eventually the expiration date passed

00:05:58.964 --> 00:06:02.167
and I begrudgingly picked up
the yogurt that was left and

00:06:02.167 --> 00:06:05.930
fringed at the thump it made when
it hit the bottom of my trash can.

00:06:07.658 --> 00:06:11.060
Now I'm going to go out on a limb here and
say that most of you all most likely have

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:14.850
had experience throwing away food
that's not too dissimilar to this.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:20.640
But I'd like to remind to the part of my
story where I thought it be too weird to

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:21.980
give the yogurt away.

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:25.182
Why is that so weird?

00:06:25.182 --> 00:06:28.391
That shouldn't be weird,
and as it happens,

00:06:28.391 --> 00:06:31.450
a lot of people don't
think it actually is.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:36.700
There's a startup, OYO, that facilitates
peer-to-peer sharing of food.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.990
At this type,
they've shared over 400,000 food items,

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:44.670
and have an exception
marketplace clearance rate.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:50.073
What this means is that
people want free food, and

00:06:50.073 --> 00:06:54.426
they're prepared to accept
it even from a stranger.

00:06:54.426 --> 00:07:00.427
The issue that OYO has found is as much as
we're prepared to accept food, we tend not

00:07:00.427 --> 00:07:06.200
to think of food that we no longer want
as something that others may still value.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:14.240
What I realize we need to do is
refrain the concept of sharing food.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:20.590
We live in a sharing economy, we share
apartments with strangers through Airbnb.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:24.170
We share our cars with others
through Uber and Lyft.

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:29.666
Why not share our leftover or
unwanted food with neighbors and friends?

00:07:31.616 --> 00:07:36.703
The second thing we need to do is be more
honest with ourselves about how much

00:07:36.703 --> 00:07:41.570
food waste we actually generate and
how we can be a part of the solution.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:46.972
No one like to thing that
they're a problematic waster.

00:07:46.972 --> 00:07:51.296
And it's much easier to think that
the issue of food waste is so

00:07:51.296 --> 00:07:55.800
big that there's nothing we can
do as individuals to impact it.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.888
But I'd like to challenge this concept.

00:07:58.888 --> 00:08:03.270
I'd like you to think about the other
issues that seem too big for

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.390
individual action to matter.

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:13.170
Recycling, energy consumption and climate
change, even civil rights movements.

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:17.120
Yes, there is more that we can
do in each of these areas, but

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.819
we've seen the cultural shifts that
have happened surrounding them.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:27.609
And they all started with individual
actions that combined have been effective.

00:08:30.232 --> 00:08:34.938
What I hope to leave you all with
today is a greater understanding for

00:08:34.938 --> 00:08:40.649
the food distribution system, and the
associated waste that goes along with it.

00:08:40.649 --> 00:08:44.750
I hope I have given you
insights to make you pause,

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:48.462
appreciate the food you
have a little more and

00:08:48.462 --> 00:08:53.850
perhaps think about ways that you
can reduce your own food waste.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:58.360
Maybe, just maybe I have
persuaded some of you to

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:01.770
stop being as weird with food as I am.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:06.680
Join me, let's all be weird together and

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:10.441
make weird the new normal.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:09:10.441 --> 00:09:10.941
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:09:10.941 --> 00:09:15.608
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:15.608 --> 00:09:19.615
[MUSIC]

